    ["comprehension", "In the wetlands passage, which creatures shelter among the reed stems?", ["Small fish", "Foxes", "Owls", "Deer", "Squirrels"], "Small fish", "The passage says that small fish shelter among the reed stems.", "select-one", "Wetlands are areas where the ground is partly or fully covered by water for some or all of the year. A reed bed may look quiet, but it can support many kinds of life. Insects feed on plants there, and small fish shelter among the stems. Some birds use the reeds for nesting and protection. Wetland plants can also slow water as it spreads across the land after heavy rain. This may reduce flooding downstream, although it cannot prevent every flood. People visit wetlands to watch wildlife, but paths and viewing points help protect fragile habitats. A visitor who stays on the marked path is less likely to disturb nests or trample young plants."],
    ["comprehension", "Which detail from the poem tells us the bakery is open?", ["The baker lifts the shutters wide.", "The buses sigh.", "The robin tests the air.", "Every window holds the light.", "The quiet has taken flight."], "The baker lifts the shutters wide.", "Opening the shutters is the detail that shows the baker is beginning work.", "select-one", "One clear bell wakes up the town,\nAnd morning lays its colours down.\nThe baker lifts the shutters wide;\nWarm cinnamon drifts outside.\nA robin tests the silver air,\nThen loops above the market square.\nThe buses sigh, the bicycles ring;\nThe streets unfold like paper wings.\nBy eight, the quiet has taken flight,\nAnd every window holds the light."]
